    "lineup",
    "nine",
    "sim"
  ],
  "text": "Draw each plan as a bar, split where its users churned. If knowing the plan told you nothing, both would split at the same height. Fifteen percent. That's independence: the chance given the plan is the same as the chance without it. Here, the split jumps from six percent to twenty-four. Plan and churn are dependent, and that gap is information you can act on. Independence is also what lets you multiply. Ten heads in a row is ten halves multiplied together... about one in a thousand. And which way round you read a conditional matters more than it looks.\n\nA disease affects one person in a thousand. A test for it is ninety-nine percent accurate, both ways. You test positive. What are the chances you have it? Your gut says ninety-nine percent. The answer is about nine. Walk a hundred thousand people through it. A hundred have the disease. Ninety-nine thousand, nine hundred don't. The test catches ninety-nine of the hundred. But it also flags one percent of the healthy by mistake. That's nine hundred and ninety-nine false alarms. Now line up everyone who tested positive. One thousand and ninety-eight people... and only ninety-nine of them are sick. That's nine percent. Simulate two million people instead, and you get nine point one.",
